From d16d488a286579217664ed9a6a2916fd36bf3c10 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: edwardzhu02 <explodingtnt@foxmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 11:35:39 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] stmp3770: on-demand paging framework (off by default, fault
 path not working)

Puts the pieces in place for running code out of a 3.5 MB window backed by
NAND, and records how far it gets:

  - stmp3770_memorymap.h: paging layout -- locked 0..0x3C000, 16 frames at
    0x3C000, data at 0x4C000, L1 at 0x78000, L2 coarse tables at 0x7C000.
  - stmp3770_map_paged() in boot.c: builds four coarse tables so sections
    0..3 hold the window; locked pages identity mapped, the rest of the
    window left invalid, data region mapped PG_DATA_VBASE -> PG_DATA_PBASE.
    The page tables sit inside the address range of the window, so those
    pages are pinned identity (window pages 60..67).
  - arch/Kconfig: PAGING_DATA_PBASE/VBASE.  Without them page.h defaults the
    data region to the start of the window, on top of it, which maps garbage
    into the first NDATA window entries.
  - arm_allocpage.c: the wrap test compared against CONFIG_LEGACY_PAGING,
    a bool, instead of CONFIG_PAGING_NPPAGED, so every page after the first
    reused frame 0.
  - hp39gii_fillpage.c: up_fillpage() maps far - PG_PAGED_VBASE onto a NAND
    blob and reads one 4 KB page through the GPMI MTD.
  - hp39gii_pagetest.c: erases blocks 5..32, writes 896 four-KB records (a
    three-instruction stub returning its own index), reads two back, then
    calls every record through the window.

Measured in EmuGII:

  - the blob round-trips: readback of pages 0 and 895 returns the written
    words (0xE59F0000 / 0xE12FFF1E / index).
  - the window is invalid before the first touch: L1[0] = 0x7C011 and
    L2[60] = 0, and the first access does take an abort.

It then fails inside the fault entry: the vector switches to SYS mode and
stores the context at the stack pointer of the faulting task, and by then
that pointer is 0x9F, so the stmia raises an alignment fault (FAR 0x9F,
FSR 1) and NuttX panics in arm_dataabort / arm_vectorprefetch.  Same on the
data path (reading the window) and on the prefetch path (calling into it),
so the problem is the fault entry, not the fill.

Kept off by default: the defconfig has the paging options commented out, so
the shipping build still boots NSH (verified pixel-identical to the MMU
build).  Next: find what clobbers sp on the way into pg_miss().
